# WP4-SIZING: issue 82 (c), and (b)'s arithmetic ahead of its hooks

`probes/read_share.py`, with tests. No model, no dependency on #81 landing.

## What is here and what is not

**(b)'s arithmetic is here; (b)'s data is not.** The share is a pure function of an alpha row and
the value vectors it weights, at the interface the ratified WP3 design already declares —
`y_t = sum_s alpha[t, s] * v_s`. So `read_share` can be written and tested today, and what #81's
hooks add is the adapter that yields those two arrays for a real point, not the measurement.

The recorded calibration artifact cannot stand in for the hooks. `alpha_calibration.json` holds
reconstruction errors and per-block and per-position statistics, not the alpha rows or the value
vectors, so the pre-check genuinely waits on #81. The stub in the tests is a stub, and it is one
because the artifact is not one.

## Three decisions in `read_share` that a reader should disagree with if they are wrong

**The share is a norm of a sum, not a sum of norms.** `|| sum_{s in span} alpha[s] v_s ||` over
`|| sum_s alpha[s] v_s ||`. Summing per-position magnitudes would bound the answer at 1 and would
answer a different question — how much weight the span carries, rather than how much of the read
it accounts for. The smallest case that separates them is in the tests: two opposed sources of
equal weight, where the magnitude reading says 0.5 and the read is zero.

**A share above 1 is a reading and is not clipped.** The span's partial sum can point against the
remainder, and then removing the span moves the read further than the read's own size. Clipping
would erase exactly that finding.

**The zero rule follows WP3's.** A read whose total norm is exactly zero has an undefined share,
marked and counted rather than scored, because a zero denominator and a zero numerator are not
the same finding.

`read_share_by_layer` reports the **maximum over heads**, not the mean. The plan's rule asks
whether the share is below a threshold *at every layer*, and a single head carrying the whole read
is the case the pre-check exists to catch; a mean over 32 heads would put it under the threshold.

## The sizing, and the one input it refuses to invent

`size_two_by_two` applies the plan's rule in the plan's own units: below 0.02 at every layer, size
against 0.05 instead, or return `NOT_WORTH_RUNNING` — **as an outcome, not an exception**, because
the plan says the second half is a real result.

It computes at `holm_alpha(alpha, arms)` for the four cells read against arm C, and it uses
`smallest_n_for_mcnemar_power`, which is a **stable crossing** rather than the first n to touch the
target. WO-STAT-001's correction records why that matters: Fisher at 3 of 3 against 0 of 3 rejects
with power 0.512, and the next attainable table at n = 4 has power 0.410, so discrete tests are
not monotone in n and "the n for 80 percent" has to mean the crossing.

**`matched_share_for` is required and has no default.** There is no measured mapping from a norm
share to a matched-only share, and putting a guess inside a sizing function is how an assumption
becomes a number nobody can find later. The Head of Interpretability owns that mapping; this
records it as an input. The test suite passes a stand-in and says at its definition that it is one
and why the constants were chosen.

Worked, with the stand-in: a pre-check negligible at every layer sizes against 0.05, which the
stand-in maps to a matched share of 0.70, giving **217 points at α/4 with power 0.8017**. Against
EXP-002's expected ~42 points, that is the number the rule exists to surface.

## The lane constraint, flagged rather than overridden

Issue 82's constraints say "Codex implements; the Deputy commits". This was assigned to me
directly, and it is instrument arithmetic rather than the experiment, so I have delivered it the
way I have delivered every slice this evening — as a patch under `pending` for the Chief's gate.
If it should go back to Codex, say so and I will hand over the design rather than the code.

